#!/bin/sh
# HOW LONG THIS HOOK TAKES IS RECORDED, because something downstream waits on it.
# `scripts/cdsfl_sv.py` commits through a subprocess with a timeout, and on
# 2026-10-17 that timeout was 60 seconds while this hook had grown to 143 --
# so `sv` could no longer save state at all, and the failure looked like a git
# problem rather than a guard set that had outgrown its waiter. A number typed
# once goes stale the next time the set grows, so the duration is MEASURED here
# and read there. The file is scratch and is not committed.
CDSFL_HOOK_T0=$(date +%s)
# ONE EXIT HANDLER, BECAUSE `trap` REPLACES RATHER THAN CHAINS. A second
# `trap ... EXIT` later in this file silently discarded the recorder, so the
# duration was never written and `sv` fell back to its floor forever -- a new
# mechanism nothing reached, which is the failure the additive standard names.
# Later cleanup registers into CDSFL_EXIT_EXTRA instead of taking the trap.
CDSFL_EXIT_EXTRA=""
# ★ B128, 2026-11-09, on his ruling: "we must be able to know why a commit
# was refused, so it can be repaired." The ledger recorded the duration and
# the exit code but NOT the cause, so a refusal in the history was a fact
# with no reason attached. Each refusal point now names itself here and the
# exit handler writes it. Empty on a clean exit, and an unnamed refusal
# records the empty string rather than failing -- this may never cost a commit.
CDSFL_REFUSAL=""
_cdsfl_on_exit() {
    _rc=$?
    if [ -n "$CDSFL_EXIT_EXTRA" ]; then
        eval "$CDSFL_EXIT_EXTRA"
    fi
    if [ -n "${CDSFL_HOOK_T0:-}" ]; then
        mkdir -p .cdsfl_tmp 2>/dev/null
        _cdsfl_secs=$(( $(date +%s) - CDSFL_HOOK_T0 ))
        # KEPT: scripts/cdsfl_sv.py:620 reads this exact path to derive its own
        # timeout, so the single number stays where it is. Nothing is removed.
        echo "$_cdsfl_secs" > .cdsfl_tmp/precommit_seconds 2>/dev/null
        # ★ AND APPENDED TO A LEDGER, 2026-11-08 on his ruling (B81). The line
        # above OVERWRITES, so this gate has never had a cost HISTORY: every
        # claim about what it costs has come from 1 number describing the last
        # run, and the header's own stated limit of 11 minutes could not be
        # checked against anything. Recent boards have run 652, 683 and 698
        # seconds, so the gate sits ON that line rather than safely inside it,
        # and a limit nobody can measure against is not a limit.
        #
        # THE OUTCOME IS RECORDED BESIDE THE DURATION, and that is the half
        # worth having: `_rc` is this trap's own exit code, so the ledger shows
        # how often the gate REFUSED as well as what it cost. A refusal leaves
        # no other trace in the repository, which is why this session's own
        # refusal count had to be session-observed rather than derived.
        #
        # IT IS A SPOOL UNDER .cdsfl_tmp AND THEREFORE UNTRACKED, DELIBERATELY.
        # A tracked ledger written from a pre-commit hook lands AFTER the commit
        # it describes, so every commit would leave the tree dirty and the
        # ledger would trail by one. Where the history should finally live is
        # part of B103, which is his decision, so this collects the evidence
        # without pre-empting it.
        #
        # IT CAN NEVER FAIL A COMMIT. Every command here is redirected and the
        # block returns nothing; an unwritable spool costs a line of history
        # and not a commit.
        printf '{"at":"%s","seconds":%s,"exit":%s,"head":"%s","why":"%s"}\n' \
            "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ 2>/dev/null)" \
            "$_cdsfl_secs" "$_rc" \
            "$(git rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null)" \
            "${CDSFL_REFUSAL:-}" \
            >> .cdsfl_tmp/precommit_durations.jsonl 2>/dev/null
    fi
    return $_rc
}
trap _cdsfl_on_exit EXIT
# CDSFL pre-commit guard. Refuses a commit when the cheap correctness guards are red.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, with the evidence rather than the intention.
# A commit-time guard was discussed on 5 occasions between 2026-08-25 and
# 2026-09-07 and was never built. On 2026-09-09 at 08:16:17 commit 57d5a0e reached
# HEAD with the full suite red at 5 failures, every one of them self-inflicted the
# previous evening: a memory file was written and `sv` -- which DERIVES the file
# ledger -- was never run. The tracker had recorded the remedy the same evening,
# in these words: "No mechanism is missing; the remedy is ordering." Ordering by
# discipline failed within the hour. This is the mechanism.
#
# WHY ONLY A HANDFUL OF FILES. Measured on this machine over 3 runs:
# 277 tests, median 7.09 s over 5 runs (range 6.39 s to 8.51 s), against
# 669.92 s for the full suite. RE-MEASURED 2026-09-10 20:2x BST: the count
# moved 178 -> 277 because `test_done_markers_carry_evidence` parametrises
# per DONE entry, and the evening closed 12 more. The gate's cost therefore
# GROWS WITH THE TASK LIST, which is worth knowing: it is not a fixed price.
# RE-MEASURED 2026-09-10 14:50 BST by scripts/precommit_gate_cost_2026-09-10.py,
# which reads this GUARDS list rather than carrying its own copy. The figure has
# now been wrong 4 times by being typed and carried forward; it is measured.
# (Was 56 tests in 1.00 to 2.02 s with 4 guards; 2 more were added
# 2026-09-10 on cc2's finding F5 and the figure is re-measured, not
# carried forward — a stale cost figure in a guard about stale claims
# would be its own joke.) A per-commit gate costing 11 minutes
# would be bypassed within a day, and a guard that gets bypassed is not a guard.
# The first 4 of these 7 files are precisely the ones that caught the
# 2026-09-09 breakage after the fact, so they are the ones that would have caught
# it before. The header said "these 4 files" until 2026-09-10 while the list held
# 7, which is the same stale-count defect the guard exists to catch.
#
# WHY IT FAILS CLOSED WHEN IT CANNOT RUN. This project's own record names "a guard
# that cannot fail is not a guard" and lists 3 instances in a single night. A hook
# that exits 0 because python was missing is that defect. So an infrastructure
# fault blocks too, loudly, and the escape hatch is explicit and deliberate:
#
#     git commit --no-verify
#
# Using it is a decision, not an accident, and that is the whole point.

set -u

REPO=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || {
    echo "pre-commit: cannot find the repository root. Refusing." >&2
    CDSFL_REFUSAL="repo-root-not-found"
    exit 1
}
cd "$REPO" || exit 1

# THE 6th AND 7th GUARDS, added 2026-09-10 on cc2's finding F5 — "the cheapest
# fix, largest effect". A commit is the exact moment a DONE marker is written,
# and the guard that checks DONE markers was collected only by the full suite,
# 13 minutes away. The hook's own header records the ruling this repeated:
# "The failure was never the absence of a rule or a checker; it was never
# running the checker."
#
# COST, MEASURED by cc2 under sh over 3 runs each: 4 guards 0.99/0.89/0.87 s,
# 6 guards 1.50/1.39/1.56 s. About +0.5 s against this hook's own stated budget
# of 1.00 to 2.02 s. The marker engine's own test comes too, which closes the
# deleted-marker case at commit time as well.
# THE 8th GUARD, added 2026-09-10 15:20 BST. `wilson_interval_consistency.py`
# caught 2 of this session's own errors within one hour -- a brief stating the
# 8-case interval against the 7-case count, and a panel seat rounding a lower
# bound UP, which narrows an interval into a stronger claim than the data
# supports. Both were caught only by the FULL suite, 14 minutes away, and an
# audit that morning had named exactly this gap: the hook does not run the test
# that is red, so a code-only commit lands a red suite silently. It did, at
# 9dfbc2e. Cost re-measured after adding it, not carried forward.
# THE 11th GUARD BLOCK, added 2026-10-18, and the reason is a RED TEST NOBODY SAW.
# `test_a_ruled_item_is_not_a_decision_2026-10-16.py` had 3 failing tests, one of
# them reporting "the severity test has left the blocker counter again -- this is
# the change he has now rejected 4 times". Measured that night: all 3 predated the
# session, the function body was byte-identical to its baseline, and the gate was
# INTACT -- the test asserted on the SOURCE TEXT of `unverified_critical_count`
# and the logic had moved into `blocking_entry_ids` during a refactor. It is now
# an executing test and 3 mutations of the real gate turn it red.
#
# IT WAS RED AND UNWATCHED BECAUSE IT WAS NOT IN THIS LIST. Measured the same
# night: this list holds 64 of the repository's 668 test files, 9.58%, and the
# last FULL-suite measurement is 8,878 passed at 1cfc30e on 2026-09-29, 382
# commits earlier. The subset is deliberate -- these are the cheap guards -- but
# a red outside it accumulates unseen, which is what happened here.
#
# ★ 29 GUARDS ADDED 2026-10-31, AND THE SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY NOT "ALL OF THEM".
# He accepted a recommendation to add the unreached test files to this gate, on
# the strength of a figure that answered a different question. The figure given
# was "the 6 newest guards run 75 tests in 18.88 seconds against a gate that
# currently takes 312 seconds, about 6 per cent" -- true, and those 6 guards are
# 75 of the 12,048 tests in the unreached set, 0.623 per cent of it. Adding
# EVERY unreached file makes this gate the full board: +12,048 tests and
# +5,321.85 s, taking 312 s to 5,634 s, a factor of 18.05. A commit that takes
# an hour and a half is a commit nobody makes, and a gate nobody can afford to
# run protects nothing. The unreached set is also 2.26x slower PER TEST
# (441.72 ms against 195.50 ms), so this list's current membership is the cheap
# files and that is unlikely to be an accident. Producer:
# scripts/what_adding_every_unreached_test_file_really_costs_2026-10-31.py.
#
# SO THE SCOPE IS THE GUARDS WRITTEN SINCE 2026-10-29, which is what the
# original argument was actually about: a guard on one of these reports sat
# failing through 3 consecutive commits because nothing cheap ran it. Measured
# 2026-10-31: these 29 files run 440 tests in 71.13 s, which takes the gate from
# 312 s to about 383 s -- 22.8 per cent, not 1,705. All 440 pass; 6 did not when
# first run together, and each of those 6 was a stale pin rather than a defect
# in the product (a citation of mine naming a seat's sandbox path, the B50
# filter pin his ruling moved, and a regression guard that needed a blocker to
# exist in the live list).
#
# The 6 files below add 75 tests, about 5% on top of the 1,348 this list already
# holds, and each guards something a founder ruling turns on: the `ruled` field
# the morning report routes on, the severity gate he has defended 4 times, the
# capability-identity loudness, the verbatim exemption that lets a seat's words
# reach the record unedited, the model-admission failure mode, and the
# human-review stop's evidence condition.
# THE 28th FILE, added 2026-10-31, and it guards an INSTRUMENT rather than the
# product. 10 tests, 2.22 s (median of 3 runs: 2.81, 2.22, 2.14), so about 0.6%
# on top of this list's 383 s.
#
# WHY AN INSTRUMENT EARNS A SLOT. The producer it guards,
# scripts/is_the_polarity_hazard_reachable_2026-10-31.py, shipped with 3 defects
# that each changed a printed figure, and 2 of them printed a CONFIDENT ZERO:
# a grep that counted the producer's own text as 5 live references, and an entry
# predicate requiring `finding_id` where the resolved registry is keyed by
# `canonical_id`, which collected 29,755 dicts whose status was None in all
# 29,755 cases and reported the exposure census as "0 and 0". A zero that is a
# zero of nothing is the failure mode this project has recorded most often.
# The guard's own first run then found a 4th: the exclusion covered only the
# producer's file, so the apparatus simply moved into the guard's docstring and
# the count went back to 5.
#
# All 3 restored defects are verified RED across 5 distinct assertions.
#
# ★ 2 MORE ADDED 2026-10-31, UNDER THE SCOPE RULE STATED ABOVE RATHER THAN AS
# AN EXCEPTION TO IT: both were written on 2026-10-31 and the scope is "the
# guards written since 2026-10-29". Measured together before adding: 65 tests
# in 12.49 s, so about 3.3 per cent on top of the 383 s this list had reached.
#   - test_the_secondary_falsifier_rule_2026-10-31.py guards the founder's
#     primary/secondary rule, including that Wolfram can never become a
#     PRIMARY falsifier in any of the 10 domain configs (his 2026-09-28
#     ruling) and that a paid route is never auto-selected as a secondary.
#   - test_the_role_vocabulary_has_forked_2026-10-31.py guards the undeclared
#     role SET as a relationship, and the finding that `mypy_check` reports OK
