# THE 8th, 9th AND 10th GUARDS, added 2026-09-29, and the reason is a MEASURED
# recurrence rather than a hunch. Across 2026-09-28 and 2026-09-29 the full suite
# was run 5 times to discover 2 classes of defect that a commit-time check catches
# in seconds:
#
#   * a script added to `scripts/` that does not answer `--help`, so the flag runs
#     the measurement. Caught twice in 24 hours, once on a script CC1 wrote and
#     once on a script CC1 adopted from a panel seat without checking.
#   * a panel round dispatched and then left unmirrored and without a FULL RECORD
#     note. Caught on 5 rounds across 2 days.
#
# Neither is a defect in the work. Both are HOUSEKEEPING THE PROJECT ALREADY
# REQUIRES, discovered 30 minutes after the commit instead of 20 seconds before it.
# The cost was measured, not estimated, on 2026-09-29:
#
#   test_help_is_answered_2026-09-11.py               9261 ms
#   test_panel_records_are_preserved_2026-09-11.py    2592 ms
#   test_panel_rounds_have_a_full_record_2026-09-11.py 942 ms
#
# about 12.8 s on top of the existing gate. `test_measurement_survey_is_safe_2026-09-11.py`
# was measured at 48230 ms and DELIBERATELY LEFT OUT: it costs 4x the other 3
# together and catches the same `--help` class the 9.3 s guard already catches. The
# header above records the ruling this respects -- "a per-commit gate costing 11
# minutes would be bypassed within a day, and a guard that gets bypassed is not a
# guard". A gate that roughly doubles from 11 s to 24 s is still a gate anyone will
# keep; one that ran for a minute might not be.

# STAGE 0 -- REFRESH THE FOUNDER'S DESKTOP COPIES. NON-BLOCKING BY DESIGN.
#
# He reads 4 files from his Desktop and treats them as current. On 2026-09-10 two
# of them had silently diverged -- the master task list 29,044 bytes short
# and 3.45 h behind, the outcomes log 5,016 bytes short -- and he asked that
# morning whether the companion file "on my desktop (which I presume you have
# been updating)" was current. It was not. The same class of staleness had
# already cost him a drive home from his hotel on 2026-09-09.
#
# THIS STAGE NEVER REFUSES A COMMIT. A convenience copy on one machine must not
# be able to block work, and a guard that fires on the ordinary case teaches
# people to reach for --no-verify. It refreshes and reports; if the Desktop is
# absent, as it is on any other machine, it says so and exits 0.
#
# WHY IT RUNS FIRST, AND IT DID NOT UNTIL 2026-09-10.
# This was the LAST stage, after the guards. One of the guards is the mirror
# drift check. So a commit that edited a mirrored file made that guard red, the
# hook exited 1, and the stage that would have repaired the drift was never
# reached -- the repair sat downstream of the check that needed it. The founder's
# master task list is a mirrored file, so from the moment the RUNWAY joined the
# guarded set every further commit on the task list was refused, permanently,
# with the fix present in the same script and unreachable.
#
# ORDER IS THE WHOLE FIX: repair first, then check. It cannot mask a real defect,
# because the repo copy is canonical by founder ruling and the refresh only ever
# copies repo -> Desktop. If the refresh itself fails or is absent, the guard
# still runs afterwards and still refuses. The check keeps its authority; it
# simply stops being the thing that prevents its own remedy.
# ─── ORDER WITHIN STAGE 0: EVERY REPAIR THAT EDITS A FILE RUNS BEFORE THE
# ─── MIRROR REFRESH, BECAUSE THE MIRROR REFRESH COPIES THOSE FILES.
#
# FOUND 2026-09-10 BY THE HOOK REFUSING THIS VERY COMMIT. The citation repair
# was added BELOW the mirror refresh. It edits the founder's master task list,
# which IS a mirrored file, so the mirror was copied and then the canonical copy
# changed underneath it -- and the mirror-drift guard, a few lines later,
# refused. That is the IDENTICAL fault the mirror refresh was moved to stage 0
# to cure earlier the same day: a repair sitting below the check that needs it.
# Adding a second repair recreated it 1 stage lower down.
#
# THE RULE, so the next addition does not repeat it a third time: a stage-0
# repair that WRITES a file goes ABOVE the mirror refresh; the mirror refresh is
# always LAST, because it is the one that copies. Held by
# bench/tests/test_precommit_stage0_order_2026-09-10.py.
#
# ─── AND A REPAIR MUST RE-STAGE WHAT IT WROTE. Found 2026-09-11 by cloning HEAD
# ─── and running the suite there: 3 failures, all of them repairs that had run.
#
# A pre-commit hook sees the STAGED snapshot. A repair that writes to the
# WORKING TREE and does not `git add` its output ships the UNREPAIRED content
# and leaves the repair sitting uncommitted, to be swept into the NEXT commit.
# So the repairs were permanently one commit behind, every clone at HEAD was
# broken, and the working tree looked perfect the whole time -- which is why it
# took a clone to see it.
#
# ONLY THE EXACT PATHS EACH REPAIR REPORTS CHANGING are staged, so an unrelated
# work-in-progress edit in the same file is never swept in.

RESTAGE_LIB="hooks/stage0_restage.sh"
if [ ! -f "$RESTAGE_LIB" ]; then
    echo "pre-commit: $RESTAGE_LIB is missing." >&2
    echo "            Without it the stage-0 repairs below write the working" >&2
    echo "            tree and never reach the commit, so every clone at HEAD" >&2
    echo "            is broken while this tree looks clean. That is the defect" >&2
    echo "            measured on 2026-09-11 (3 failures in a fresh clone, 0" >&2
    echo "            here). Refusing rather than reintroducing it silently." >&2
    CDSFL_REFUSAL="stage0-restage-helper-missing"
    exit 1
fi
. "$RESTAGE_LIB"
cdsfl_warn_if_partial_commit
# BEFORE ANY REPAIR WRITES. Copies the working-tree content of every path that
# is modified in BOTH the index and the worktree, so a repair to such a file can
# be staged as a DELTA rather than by `git add`, which would take the hunks the
# author withheld. Normally an empty set and 2 git calls.
cdsfl_snapshot_partially_staged

# THE SAME REPAIR-BEFORE-CHECK ORDERING, FOR LINE CITATIONS. Added 2026-09-10,
# task A2.
#
# WHAT WAS WRONG. Notes cite the runner as `reference_runner_v3.py:11055`, and
# `scripts/citation_content_guard_2026-09-10.py` checks that such a citation
# lands INSIDE the symbol it names. Editing the runner moves every symbol below
# the edit, so the citations go stale by exactly the number of lines added --
# measured 2026-09-10: 6 of 6 anchored citations were outside their symbol, off
# by +27, +47 and +22, and the guard was red in a fresh clone AND in the
# maintainer's tree. It had been red since some earlier runner edit that nobody
# connected to it.
#
# The guard already shipped the cure: `--fix` rewrites the line number to the
# symbol's first line. NOTHING RAN IT. That is the additive standard's symmetric
# half -- an addition nothing reaches -- sitting inside the repair for another
# instance of the same shape 20 lines above.
#
# LIKE THE MIRROR REFRESH, THIS NEVER REFUSES. It repairs and reports. The guard
# still runs afterwards in the suite and still fails on anything the repair could
# not fix -- a citation naming a symbol that no longer exists, for instance,
# which is a real defect and not a stale offset.
# THE RUN LEDGER IS DERIVED AND CITES THE RUNNER BY LINE NUMBER, so any runner
# edit makes it stale and its drift guard red. Same ordering, same reasoning as
# the 2 stages around it. `--refresh` DECLINES on a checkout holding fewer
# artefacts than the ledger declares, so a clone cannot shrink it.
if [ -f scripts/experiment_run_ledger.py ]; then
    _LEDGER_OUT=$(python3 scripts/experiment_run_ledger.py --refresh 2>&1 || true)
    # `REFUSED|Traceback|Error` TOO. A traceback contains none of the success
    # tokens and `|| true` eats the exit code, so a repair that CRASHED printed
    # nothing at all and the commit went through with the repair not done --
    # which is the defect this whole stage exists to close, re-entering through
    # its own reporting. Found by the fable seat, round 14.
    echo "$_LEDGER_OUT" | grep -E "refreshed|NOT refreshed|REFUSED|Traceback|Error" \
        | sed 's/^/pre-commit: /' || true
    echo "$_LEDGER_OUT" | cdsfl_restage_from_ledger_output
fi

# ── THE MEMORY EXCLUSIONS LEDGER, REPAIRED BEFORE IT IS CHECKED ────────────
# The 4th repair stage, added 2026-11-07, for the SAME reason the 3 above it run
# before the guards and not after: this ledger's 2 drift guards live in the board
# below, and the derivation that satisfies them lived only inside a full `sv`,
# which commits and pushes and so cannot be called from here.
#
# MEASURED COST: on 2026-11-07 writing a memory file outside `sv` drifted the
# ledger twice in one session, 322 -> 323 and 324 -> 325, and each drift refused
# a commit AFTER the 11-minute board had already run. The ledger's own file
# records 7 consecutive manual corrections before those 2. The guards were right
# every time; the board time was wasted every time.
#
# `|| true` AND THE ERROR TOKENS TOGETHER, copied from the stage below after the
# fable seat found that a repair which CRASHED printed nothing and let the commit
# through. A traceback carries none of the success tokens.
if [ -f scripts/refresh_memory_ledger_2026-11-07.py ]; then
    _MEM_OUT=$(python3 scripts/refresh_memory_ledger_2026-11-07.py 2>&1 || true)
    echo "$_MEM_OUT" | grep -E "refreshed|already current|REFUSED|Traceback|Error" \
        | sed 's/^/pre-commit: /' || true
    case "$_MEM_OUT" in
        *refreshed*) git add resources/MEMORY_EXCLUSIONS.md 2>/dev/null || true ;;
    esac
fi

if [ -f scripts/citation_content_guard_2026-09-10.py ]; then
    _CIT_OUT=$(python3 scripts/citation_content_guard_2026-09-10.py --fix 2>&1 || true)
    echo "$_CIT_OUT" | grep -E "repaired|->|REFUSED|Traceback|Error" \
        | sed 's/^/pre-commit: /' || true
    echo "$_CIT_OUT" | cdsfl_restage_from_citation_output
fi


cdsfl_snapshot_cleanup

if [ -f scripts/sync_desktop_mirrors.py ]; then
    if ! python3 scripts/sync_desktop_mirrors.py 2>&1 | sed 's/^/pre-commit: /'; then
        echo "pre-commit: the Desktop mirror refresh failed; commit continues." >&2
    fi
fi

MISSING=""
for f in $GUARDS; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || MISSING="$MISSING $f"
done
if [ -n "$MISSING" ]; then
    echo "pre-commit: guard file(s) missing:$MISSING" >&2
    echo "pre-commit: refusing rather than passing on an absent check." >&2
    CDSFL_REFUSAL="guard-file-missing"
    exit 1
fi

command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
    echo "pre-commit: python3 not on PATH, so the guards cannot run. Refusing." >&2
    echo "pre-commit: bypass deliberately with: git commit --no-verify" >&2
    CDSFL_REFUSAL="python3-not-on-path"
    exit 1
}

# ★ N4, 2026-11-09. HOISTED ABOVE THE BOARD. This block used to run AFTER
# the 235-guard board, so a commit refused by a 1-second lint first paid for a
# board whose mean cost is 1615.30 s over 10 recorded runs. All the cheap
# checks together are bounded at 27.66 s, which is 0.0172 of a refused board,
# a ratio of 217.0651; the ledger records 54.2 minutes already spent that way.
#
# WHY HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE, and both constraints were checked by execution:
#   (1) It must stay AFTER stage 0, which writes and stages files inside this
#       block's own glob -- an in-run data dependency, not a style choice.
#   (2) It may not go INSIDE the stage-0 window, because
#       bench/tests/test_desktop_mirrors_stay_current_2026-09-10.py slices
#       `# STAGE 0 --` to `MISSING=""` and asserts that slice holds exactly 1
#       `exit 1`; this block holds 3.
#   (3) It must stay AFTER the python3-availability check above, which it now
#       immediately follows.
# That leaves exactly 1 legal position, and this is it. The block is MOVED,
# not changed: 0 of its lines were edited.
#
# NO DEPENDENCY ON THE BOARD, verified rather than assumed: the only names it
# reads and does not set are `_rc` (its own helper), `f` (its own loop) and
# `LINT_CODE`, which occurs solely inside a comment.
# ── 5th guard: LINT THE NOTES BEING COMMITTED ────────────────────────────────
#
# WHY, and it is the founder's own ruling rather than an idea. On 2026-09-04 he
# wrote, after the spelled-number rule had been stated and re-violated
# repeatedly: "THE RULE ALREADY EXISTED ... and the linter already caught it ...
# The failure was never the absence of a rule or a checker; it was never running
# the checker. Therefore: run `python3 scripts/note_vagueness_lint.py <file>` on
# every note and every TTS file BEFORE delivering it, and treat a spelled-number
# finding as blocking rather than advisory."
#
# WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WIRED BEFORE THIS. The linter's LOGIC is exercised by 3 test
# files, which is not the same thing as running it over the notes. The only test
# that lints real notes selects those declaring a v1.7 foot-line: measured
# 2026-09-09, 29 of 379 files under experimental_notes, 7.65%, Wilson
# [5.4%, 10.8%]. A new note omitting the foot-line was exempt from the check by
# omission -- the checker existed, and the note simply never met it.
#
# WHY THE ADDED LINES ONLY. Linting 379 archival notes on every commit would fire
# on work written under earlier standards, which the standard itself preserves
# deliberately. Linting whole STAGED FILES is barely narrower: correcting 1 number
# in a 400-line note would hold the commit to all 400. What a commit can fairly be
# held to is what that commit ADDS, and that is what is linted below.
#
# WHY IT REFUSES RATHER THAN WARNS. The founder's ruling says blocking, not
# advisory. `git commit --no-verify` remains the deliberate escape.
#
# ARCHIVED SEAT EVIDENCE IS EXCLUDED, added 2026-10-02, and the reason is that
# the alternative is editing evidence. `experimental_notes/seat_evidence/` holds
# documents a PANEL SEAT wrote, preserved unedited so a clone can reach them,
# with each file's sha256 recorded before its provenance header. Rescuing the
# 2026-09-20 to 2026-09-30 backlog staged 8 such notes carrying 17 findings
# between them -- unnamed subjects and valueless quantities in a seat's own
# prose -- and the ratchet refused the commit.
#
# Editing them to pass would destroy the thing that makes them evidence, which
# is the same rule already applied to a seat's raw reply in a FULL RECORD: the
# figure is corrected in the readable record and the reply is left exactly as
# it arrived. The note standard governs what THIS PROJECT writes for its own
# readers; it has no authority over what another model wrote inside a sandbox.
#
# NOT A HOLE: the exclusion is one directory whose contents are byte-pinned by
# `test_seat_evidence_reaches_a_clone_2026-10-01.py`, so a project note cannot
# be hidden there, and every other note under experimental_notes is still
# judged. Same shape, and the same justification, as the prefix exemption in
# test_every_test_file_is_collected_2026-09-01.py.
STAGED_NOTES=$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM \
    | grep -E '^experimental_notes/.*\.md$' \
    | grep -v '^experimental_notes/seat_evidence/' || true)

if [ -n "$STAGED_NOTES" ]; then
    if [ ! -f scripts/note_vagueness_lint.py ]; then
        echo "pre-commit: the note linter is missing. Refusing rather than" >&2
        echo "            passing on an absent check." >&2
        CDSFL_REFUSAL="note-linter-missing"
        exit 1
    fi
    # A RATCHET, NOT A BAR. Refuse only findings the commit INTRODUCES.
    #
    # This took 2 attempts to scope and both failures are worth recording,
    # because they are the same mistake at different widths. Linting whole
    # staged FILES refused its own first real commit with 388 findings, every
    # one pre-existing prose in archival notes that a 1-line number correction
    # had touched. Linting ADDED LINES cut that to 38 and was still wrong: a
    # changed line appears whole in the diff, so correcting 1 numeral in a
    # sentence made the commit answerable for that sentence's decade of prose.
    #
    # The question a guard should ask is not "is this file clean" but "did this
    # commit make it worse", which is the ratchet idiom this project already
    # uses for unreached config fields. Each staged note is linted at HEAD and
    # as staged, and only an INCREASE refuses. A note written under an earlier
    # standard can be corrected without being rewritten; a new violation cannot
    # be added.
    #
    # A guard that fires on the ordinary case teaches people to reach for
    # --no-verify by reflex, and the 2 earlier scopings would have taught that
    # on their first outing.
    # PER FILE, NOT ON TOTALS — and the difference is a demonstrated bypass.
    #
    # The first ratchet summed every staged note and compared totals. A reviewer
    # reproduced the hole on 2026-09-10: one commit that cleaned 3 spelled
    # numbers out of an archival note AND added a brand-new note containing
    # "twenty-nine" nets -2, so the total fell and the commit landed, exit 0.
    # The guard SAW the new violation and printed it by name, then let it
    # through. Both this hook's own comment that "a NEW note is held to the whole
    # standard" and its test's docstring that "the ratchet must not become an
    # amnesty for new work" were false in that path, because the only test for it
    # committed a single file and never reached the mixed case.
    #
    # A ratchet on a SUM is not a ratchet. Each file is now judged on its own,
    # and any single file that gets worse refuses the commit.
    WORSENED=""
    WORSENED_COUNT=0
    BEFORE_TOTAL=0
    AFTER_TOTAL=0
    LINT_BROKE=""
    STAGED_COPY=$(mktemp -d -t cdsfl_note_lint)
    # REGISTERED, NOT TRAPPED. This used to be `trap 'rm -rf ...' EXIT`, which
    # REPLACED the hook's own exit handler and so discarded the duration record
    # that `scripts/cdsfl_sv.py` sizes its commit timeout from.
    CDSFL_EXIT_EXTRA="rm -rf \"$STAGED_COPY\"; $CDSFL_EXIT_EXTRA"

    # THE LINTER'S OWN EXIT CODE, CAPTURED WHERE IT RUNS.
    #
    # `LINT_CODE=$?` used to sit after an if/else, so it read the status of the
    # if/else — which always succeeds — and the "linter could not run" refusal
    # was unreachable dead code. Demonstrated:
    #   sh -c 'A=5;B=3; if [ "$A" -gt "$B" ]; then F=$((A-B)); else F=0; fi; \
    #          LINT_CODE=$?; echo $LINT_CODE'  ->  0
    # A linter that errors prints "0 finding(s)" on its way out, so the count
    # read 0 and the commit passed. That is failing OPEN in a guard whose own
    # header says it fails closed.
    # $2 IS THE LOGICAL PATH, and passing it closed a hole measured 2026-10-17.
    # This function lints a COPY under a mktemp directory. A temporary path is
    # under neither `experimental_notes/` nor a `*_tts/` folder, so every
    # path-sensitive rule -- above all the note-standard stamp -- returned
    # nothing for BOTH sides of every comparison. The stamp rule therefore never
    # participated in the ratchet, and a note could lose its stamp with nothing
    # to say so. `--as` tells the linter what the bytes REPRESENT.
    _lint_count() {   # $1 = file to read; $2 = logical path; echoes the count
        _out=$(python3 scripts/note_vagueness_lint.py --as "$2" "$1" 2>&1); _rc=$?
        printf '%s\n' "$_out" | grep -oE '^[[:space:]]*[0-9]+ finding' \
            | tail -1 | grep -oE '[0-9]+'
        return $_rc
    }

    for f in $STAGED_NOTES; do
        base=$(basename "$f")
        git show ":$f" > "$STAGED_COPY/after_$base" 2>/dev/null || continue

        A=$(_lint_count "$STAGED_COPY/after_$base" "$f") || LINT_BROKE="$LINT_BROKE $f(staged)"
        if git show "HEAD:$f" > "$STAGED_COPY/before_$base" 2>/dev/null; then
            B=$(_lint_count "$STAGED_COPY/before_$base" "$f") || LINT_BROKE="$LINT_BROKE $f(HEAD)"
        else
            B=0   # a NEW note has no baseline and is held to the whole standard
        fi

        AFTER_TOTAL=$((AFTER_TOTAL + ${A:-0}))
        BEFORE_TOTAL=$((BEFORE_TOTAL + ${B:-0}))

        if [ "${A:-0}" -gt "${B:-0}" ]; then
            WORSENED_COUNT=$((WORSENED_COUNT + 1))
            WORSENED="$WORSENED
  $f: ${B:-0} -> ${A:-0} finding(s)"
            echo "" >&2
            echo "pre-commit: $f went from ${B:-0} to ${A:-0} note finding(s)." >&2
            python3 scripts/note_vagueness_lint.py --as "$f" "$STAGED_COPY/after_$base" 2>&1 \
                | tail -20 >&2
        fi
    done

    # FAIL CLOSED WHEN THE CHECKER ITSELF BROKE. Checked before the verdict,
    # because a count read off a failed run is not a count.
    if [ -n "$LINT_BROKE" ]; then
        echo "" >&2
        echo "pre-commit: REFUSED. The note linter exited non-zero on:$LINT_BROKE" >&2
        echo "pre-commit: a count read off a failed run is not a count." >&2
        echo "pre-commit: to commit anyway, deliberately: git commit --no-verify" >&2
        CDSFL_REFUSAL="note-linter-errored"
        exit 1
    fi

    if [ "$WORSENED_COUNT" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo "" >&2
        echo "pre-commit: REFUSED. $WORSENED_COUNT staged note(s) got WORSE:$WORSENED" >&2
        echo "" >&2
        echo "pre-commit: judged per file, not on the total. A commit that cleans" >&2
        echo "            one note may not smuggle a new violation into another." >&2
        echo "pre-commit: founder ruling 2026-09-04 makes these blocking, not advisory." >&2
        echo "pre-commit: to commit anyway, deliberately: git commit --no-verify" >&2
        CDSFL_REFUSAL="note-lint-ratchet-worsened"
        exit 1
    fi
    echo "pre-commit: note lint ratchet held ($BEFORE_TOTAL at HEAD, $AFTER_TOTAL as staged)."
fi


OUT=$(python3 -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider $GUARDS 2>&1)
CODE=$?
if [ "$CODE" -ne 0 ]; then
    echo "" >&2
    echo "pre-commit: REFUSED. The cheap guards are red." >&2
    echo "" >&2
    # THE FAILURE, NOT THE LAST 25 LINES. This printed `tail -25`, and on this
    # machine pytest's last 25 lines are TMPDIR cleanup warnings from unrelated
    # tests -- 25 lines of `rm_rf` noise and 0 lines naming what broke. Twice in
    # one session the real cause had to be found by re-running the guards by
    # hand, which is the hook costing time instead of saving it. The FAILED and
    # ERROR lines and the assertion text come first; the tail is kept as a
    # fallback for a failure that produces neither.
    # ★ THE NAMES ARE PRINTED SEPARATELY FROM THE DETAIL, AND UNCAPPED, 2026-11-09.
    # This was 1 grep for '^(FAILED|ERROR)|^E  ' piped through `head -20`, and
    # that cap structurally dropped the thing a reader needs most. pytest emits
    # `E  ` traceback lines FIRST and its `FAILED` short summary LAST, so on a
    # board with more than about 2 failures the 20 lines were consumed entirely
    # by tracebacks and NOT ONE failing test was named. Measured on 2026-11-09:
    # a refusal reporting `9 failed` named 0 of the 9, and the other 7 had to be
    # hunted by re-running guards by hand -- which is the same cost this block's
    # own comment below was written to prevent, recurring because the fix capped
    # a stream it did not order. 2 greps: every name, then bounded detail.
    _NAMES=$(printf '%s\n' "$OUT" | grep -E '^(FAILED|ERROR)')
    _DETAIL=$(printf '%s\n' "$OUT" | grep -E '^E  ' | head -20)
    if [ -n "$_NAMES" ] || [ -n "$_DETAIL" ]; then
        if [ -n "$_NAMES" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$_NAMES" >&2; echo "" >&2; fi
        if [ -n "$_DETAIL" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$_DETAIL" >&2; fi
        printf '%s\n' "$OUT" | grep -E '^[0-9]+ (passed|failed)|failed,' | tail -1 >&2
    else
        printf '%s\n' "$OUT" | tail -25 >&2
    fi
    echo "" >&2
    echo "pre-commit: the usual cause is a memory file written without running sv," >&2
    echo "            which DERIVES the file ledger. Try:" >&2
    echo "              python3 -c \"import sys,pathlib; sys.path.insert(0,'scripts'); import cdsfl_sv as sv; sv._update_memory_exclusions_ledger(pathlib.Path('.').resolve())\"" >&2
    echo "" >&2
    echo "pre-commit: to commit anyway, deliberately: git commit --no-verify" >&2
    CDSFL_REFUSAL="desktop-mirror-drifted"
    exit 1
fi

printf '%s\n' "$OUT" | tail -1

# ── THE BOARD'S SCOPE TRAVELS WITH THE BOARD'S FIGURE ────────────────────────
#
# Added 2026-11-04 because the line above was read as the suite and reported to
# the founder as a fully green board, in answer to his explicit instruction not
# to cut corners to make it green. It is the GUARDS list only. Measured the same
# day by `scripts/the_gate_board_is_not_the_suite_2026-11-04.py`: 2,540 of 15,431
# collected tests AS AT 2026-11-04 08:30 GMT, a share of 0.164604, so 12,891 tests
# are never run here and a regression outside the list can sit unnoticed. One had
# -- red for 26 commits. THE PAIR IS A SNAPSHOT AND MOVES WHENEVER A TEST IS ADDED:
# it read 2,532 of 15,423 two hours earlier. Quote it only with its stamp, or run
# the producer. The FILE share below is computed live and needs no stamp.
#
# This is the `feedback_true_sentence_wrong_frame` shape for the 2nd time: an
# earlier report of "2,333 passed" was 120 files of 759. A figure whose scope is
# not attached to it WILL be requoted without the scope, so the scope is printed
# here, beside the number, where a reader cannot separate them.
#
# IT COUNTS FILES, NOT TESTS, DELIBERATELY. A test-count share needs a full
# collection, which measured 8.45 s -- a cost on every commit, to restate a
# figure whose producer is named below. The file share is a glob and is free.
_GUARD_FILES=$(printf '%s\n' "$GUARDS" | grep -c '[^[:space:]]')
_SUITE_FILES=$(ls bench/tests/test_*.py 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
echo "pre-commit: that board is the gate's ${_GUARD_FILES} guard files of ${_SUITE_FILES} in bench/tests/ -- NOT the suite."
echo "            For the test-count share: python3 scripts/the_gate_board_is_not_the_suite_2026-11-04.py"

exit 0
